1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,128
Start by counting whether each P is followed by a QRS. Then compare PR
intervals in conducted beats. The school decision tree is a memory
aid, but its first constant-PR question must not make you overlook
dropped impulses that also have a constant PR when they do conduct.

2
00:00:19,128 --> 00:00:36,576
An origin problem asks where the impulse starts. AV block asks whether
atrial impulses reach the ventricles. Delay may be nodal, while
important block can lie below the AV node. A longer strip is often
needed to establish the relationship.

3
00:00:36,576 --> 00:00:56,016
First-degree AV block describes prolonged AV transmission with one-to-
one conduction. In the school reference, PR exceeds zero point two
seconds and stays relatively consistent. It is not an intermittently
missing QRS, and not a situation where atria and ventricles are
independent.

4
00:00:56,016 --> 00:01:14,952
Review vagal influences, AV-nodal medication exposure and clinical
context. A stable asymptomatic patient may need observation and
communication rather than rate-directed intervention. New symptoms,
ischemic concern or progression change the urgency and require
reassessment.

5
00:01:14,952 --> 00:01:32,304
In the classic Mobitz-one pattern, PR progressively lengthens until
one atrial impulse fails to conduct, then the cycle resets. Identify
the nonconducted P, not merely a long RR gap. Grouped beating may help
you notice the recurring pattern.

6
00:01:32,304 --> 00:01:52,704
Mobitz one is commonly associated with AV-nodal behaviour, vagal tone,
medications or inferior ischemia. It is often better tolerated than
distal block, but symptoms and poor perfusion still demand action
through the appropriate pathway. A familiar benign association is not
a substitute for assessment.

7
00:01:52,704 --> 00:02:13,152
Mobitz two has intermittently nonconducted atrial impulses without the
progressive PR prolongation of classic Wenckebach. The conducted
intervals can look entirely normal. Important disease is often below
the AV node in the His–Purkinje system, rather than simply a more
severe nodal delay.

8
00:02:13,152 --> 00:02:32,592
This pattern carries concern for progression and an unreliable
ventricular rate. Escalate early and communicate the relationship and
the patient’s condition. Do not turn the school phrase about cautious
atropine into an independent drug rule; the current authorized
bradycardia pathway determines treatment.

9
00:02:32,592 --> 00:02:49,104
In a two-to-one pattern, every other atrial impulse fails to conduct.
You may not have enough consecutive conducted intervals to see whether
PR is progressively lengthening. Therefore the fixed ratio alone
cannot prove Mobitz one or Mobitz two.

10
00:02:49,104 --> 00:03:06,912
Report the actual atrial and ventricular rates, the conduction ratio,
QRS width and clinical status. State the subtype uncertainty rather
than guessing. A classification limit should increase attention to
monitoring and expert review, not create reassurance.

11
00:03:06,912 --> 00:03:26,976
In complete AV block, atrial impulses do not conduct to govern
ventricular activation. P waves and escape QRS complexes follow
independent schedules. A changing apparent PR reflects that lack of
relationship; compare many cycles rather than using one accidental
alignment.

12
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:48,480
The escape origin affects ventricular rate and QRS width, but a narrow
escape is not automatically safe. Check perfusion, ischemic symptoms
and progression. Coronary supply, degenerative disease and medication
toxicity can contribute, and symptoms require prompt authorized
support and conveyance.

13
00:03:48,480 --> 00:04:10,200
Pacing can provide activation when the native rate or conduction
cannot sustain adequate circulation. The school introduces high-grade
blocks and sinus-node dysfunction as examples. Recognition should help
you anticipate deterioration and request appropriate resources, not
infer that every rhythm below a fixed rate must be paced.

14
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:32,808
BCEHS separates provider levels for bradycardia care, listing advanced
medicines and transcutaneous pacing under ACP interventions. Follow
your current service authorization and instructor direction. This ECG-
recognition chapter does not grant new PCP authority or teach a stand-
alone pacing procedure.

15
00:04:32,808 --> 00:04:53,112
Atrial pacing may produce a spike before a P wave; ventricular pacing
commonly produces a spike followed by a broad complex. Some modern
pacing signals are small or not obvious. Do not assume every paced
beat must show the same large visible spike or identical QRS
morphology.

16
00:04:53,112 --> 00:05:13,488
Electrical capture means appropriate depolarization follows the
stimulus. Mechanical capture requires evidence of a corresponding
effective pulse and circulation. Correlate pulse, pressure and the
overall patient; visible spikes or skeletal-muscle twitching are not
sufficient evidence of cardiac output.

17
00:05:13,488 --> 00:05:33,720
Failure to capture means a pacing stimulus is not followed by the
expected cardiac depolarization. Failure to sense may cause stimuli to
occur inappropriately in relation to intrinsic beats. Both demand
clinical correlation; do not attempt to reprogram an implanted device
from a rhythm-recognition lesson.

18
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:50,712
Ask why the device was implanted and when it was last checked, if
circumstances permit. Record symptoms, device information, actual
pulse correlation and suspected malfunction. A normal paced rate does
not rule out poor perfusion or another acute disease.
